THM: Linux Fundamentals Part 1
  whoami - tells which user is logged in being used
  echo - display text on screen. Put in quotes for multiple words.
  ls - lists whats in the current folder
  cd - change directory
  cat - shows contents of a file
  pwd - print working directory (file path)
  find - search for files by name (find -name passwords.txt)
  grep - searches inside for specific text (grep "password123" passwords.txt)

  Operators
  & - runs the command in the background, doesn't wait to complete
  && - runs both commands, but waits for the 1st to finish before running the 2nd
  > - redirects output, will overwrite
  >> - redirects output, doesn't overwrite it just adds to whats already there

THM: Linux Fundamentals Part 2
  ssh - remoting into another pc (ssh username@10.66.184.180)

  Arguments
  -a - shows all, such as hidden folders.
  . - hidden files and folders
  --help - lists the possible options that the command accepts
  touch - create file (touch recipe) (creates a file named recipe)
  mkdir - create a folder (mkdir mydirectory) (creates a folder named mydirectory)
  cp - copy a file or folder (cp recipe recipe2) (copies entire file contents from recipe to recipe2)
  mv - move a file or folder (mv recipe recipe2) (renames recipe to recipe2 with original file contents)
  rm - remove a file or folder (rm -R mydirectory) (-R is required)
  / - root
  /etc - root directory where system and program files are stored
  /var - stores data for applications and services (/var/logs for example)
  /root - home directory for the root user
  /tmp - stores temporary small data

  Access (Read/Write/Execute) (owner, group, others)
  rw-r--r-- 1  : owner can read + write, group read only, others read only
  rw-rw-r-- 1  : owner can read + write, group read only, others read only
  drwxrwxr -x 2 : d means its a directory, rwx means owner and group get read write execute, others get r-x
  r-- : read only
  rw- : read + write

  su - switch user
    su - l  - creates a shell similar to a regular user accessing the system (su -l user2)

